## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) derived magnetic carbon-based nanocomposites have drawn widespread attentions due to the well distributed nanocrystals in carbon matrix. Dynamically observing the formation process is urgently needed. Herein, taking zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF)-67 as an example, the pyrolysis process is investigated by in-situ transmission electron microscopy (TEM) assisted with ex-situ characterizations. Co nanocrystals are evenly distributed in carbon at the initial stage of carbonization. By increasing pyrolysis temperature, the nanocrystals grow bigger and migrate to carbon surface. The carbon texture transfers from amorphous to crystalline at \(600\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and thoroughly converts at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In-situ heating TEM shows that more tiny Co nanocrystals move out from the carbon texture by increasing temperature from 700 to \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). At \(1{,}000\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), some escaped tiny Co nanocrystals are volatilized and disappeared. The residual escaped Co nanocrystals catalyze the formation of carbon nanotubes (CNTs). Due to the synergistic effect between Co and carbon as well as porous structure, the nanocomposites show high-efficient microwave absorption performance, which can be tuned by pyrolysis temperature, heating rate, and mass fraction. When the mass fraction is 30 wt.%, the nanocomposites obtained at 600 or \(700\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) display remarkable microwave absorption with optimal reflection loss (RL) smaller than \(-70\) dB and effective absorption band larger than 4.9 GHz. Combining the in-situ and ex-situ techniques, some key findings were observed: (1) graphitization of carbon; (2) volatilization of Co nanocrystals; (3) formation process of CNTs by Co catalyst. These findings are helpful to understand the formation of MOFs derived carbon-based composites and expand their practical applications, especially for microwave absorption.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, Co/N-doped carbon nanocomposites, in-situ transmission electron microscopy (TEM), microwave absorption
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an emerging class of porous crystalline organic–inorganic hybrid materials which are constructed by metal-containing nodes and organic linkers through coordination [1–3]. Owing to the multitudinous metal nodes and organic linkers as well as well-ordered open frameworks, MOFs can be used as sacrificial templates for the fabrication of carbon-based derivatives embedded with versatile compositions. These MOFs derivatives behave superior electrical conductivity and thermal stability, greatly expanding the applications of MOFs [4–8]. Compared to the conventional synthetic routes of carbon-based composites, the MOFs-derived carbon-based materials have remarkable merits. First, the inherent variety of MOFs affords plenty of choices to expand the carbon-based composites library. The structure and composition of
carbon-based derivatives can be determined by metal nodes, organic ligands, and pyrolysis conditions, which provides the opportunity to delicately control the chemical and physical properties. Second, the carbon-based derivatives can inherit the structure and morphology of pristine MOFs, possessing morphological advantages with high porosity and surface area. Third, the inorganic nanoparticles can be uniformly encapsulated in the carbon matrix. These advantages make MOFs derived carbon-based composites have great applications in the fields of energy conversion/storage, batteries, environmental protection, photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, and so on [9–14]. Especially, MOFs derived porous magnetic carbon-based composites have drawn widespread attentions [15–17]. Compared with the traditional magnetic carbon-based composites [18–20], the magnetic nanoparticles are much more uniformly distributed, which can hinder the aggregation caused by magnetic interaction.
This can sufficiently emerge the dielectric loss and magnetic loss capacities, leading to boosting microwave absorption performance. Besides, the magnetic nanoparticles are usually wrapped by carbon, which can protect the magnetic material from corrosion and oxidation, improving the environmental suitability. These features make porous magnetic carbon-based derivatives good candidates as bifunctional magnetic/dielectric microwave absorbents [16, 21, 22].
As one of the famous zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), ZIF-67 is topologically formed by coordinating \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) with 2-methylimidazole linker, which is widely used as the parent MOFs to fabricate porous magnetic \( \mathrm{Co/C} \) composites [23–25]. The MOFs derivatives are mainly prepared through a high-temperature carbonization process in different annealing atmospheres. For example, Kuang et al. reported the fabrication of porous \( \mathrm{Co/C} \) nanocomposites by thermal decomposition of ZIF-67 as microwave absorbent [26]; Huang's group prepared ZIF-67 derived porous \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) hollow dodecahedra as anode material for lithium-ion batteries. We considered that the carbonization parameters and process have a great influence on the formation MOFs derivatives, which can affect their properties and applications [27].
Understanding the formation process of MOFs derived carbon-based composites is of great significance to control the physicochemical properties, optimize the preparation technologies, and expand their applications. Nowadays, numerous works demonstrate that the experimental parameters of ex-situ pyrolysis process have great influence on the microstructures and properties of MOF derivatives [4, 28–30]. Dynamically in-situ observing the pyrolysis process of MOFs is helpful to understand the formation process of MOFs derivatives. However, the relative first-hand data is still lacked.
Herein, taking ZIF-67 derived \( \mathrm{Co/N} \)-doped carbon \( \left(\mathrm{Co/NC}\right) \) nanocomposites as an example, the formation process is studied by combining the ex-situ pyrolysis process and in-situ transmission electron microscopy (TEM) technique. Several key findings about the pyrolysis process, including the growth of Co nanocrystals, graphitization of carbon, volatilization of Co nanocrystals, and formation of carbon nanotubes (CNTs), are dynamically observed by in-situ transmission electron microscopy. Furthermore, the microwave absorption performance was investigated.
## 2 Experimental sections
## 2.1 Synthesis of ZIF-67
ZIF-67 was prepared by a modified room-temperature precipitation route [26, 27]. Typically, \( \mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) (8 mmol) and 2-methylimidazole (32 mmol) were dissolved in 60 and 6 mL methanol, respectively. The 2-methylimidazole contained methanol solution was dropwise added into the \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) contained methanol solution under magnetically stirring. Then the mixed solution was aged at room temperature for 48 h. The purple precipitate was collected by centrifugation, and washed by methanol and alcohol for three times.
## 2.2 Synthesis of porous Co/NC nanocomposites
The porous Co/NC nanocomposites were prepared by pyrolysis of ZIF-67 precursors in Ar atmosphere at different temperatures. The heating rate was \( 2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1} \) and the duration time was 3 h. By controlling the pyrolysis temperature to be 500, 600, 700, 800, and \( 1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), the samples were named as Co/NC-500, Co/NC-600, Co/NC-700, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000, respectively.
## 2.3 In-situ heating TEM characterization of ZIF-67
The in-situ structural evolution of ZIF-67 was observed by imageaberration-corrected FEI Spectra 300 microscope (Thermo Scientific, USA) with an accelerating voltage of 300 kV. The observations were achieved by loading the sample on a silicon nitride E-chips, which was equipped into a heating holder (Fusion 350, Protochips, USA). The sample was heating at 200, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 1,000, and \( 1{,}100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for 10 min with a heating rate of \( 10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1} \). The dose rate and screen current for the in-situ heating TEM experiment were about \( 3.02\;\mathrm{eV}\,\mathrm{m}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) and \( 0.314\;\mathrm{nA} \), respectively.
## 2.4 Characterizations
The crystalline structure was examined by X-ray diffraction instrument (XRD, DX-2700) with Cu \( \mathrm{K}\alpha \) radiation. The morphology was characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Apreo S, ThermoFisher Scientific) and transmission electron microscopy (Tecnai \( \mathrm{G}^{2} \) F20, FEI). The elemental states were analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB210). The disorder of carbon was examined by Raman spectra (in Via Reinishaw confocal spectrometer) with a laser of 633 nm. The decomposition process of ZIF-67 was recorded by thermo-gravimetric analyzer (TGA, NETZSCH STA 449F3) under Ar atmosphere in 30–1,100 °C with a heating rate of \( 10\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1} \). The \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) absorption/desorption isotherms were examined by a JW-BK100 instrument (Beijing JWGB Sci. & Tech. Co., Ltd.) at 77 K. Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method was used to identify the surface areas. The magnetic properties were measured by a superconducting quantum interference device (SQUID, MPMS-XL, Quantum Design) at room temperature.
## 2.5 Electromagnetic measurements
The microwave absorption properties of Co/NC nanocomposites were investigated through a network analyzer (Agilent Technologies E8363B) in 0.1–18 GHz. The composites for microwave absorption measurement were fabricated by mixing paraffin with 30 wt.% of Co/NC nanocomposites and pressed into a toroidal shape \( \left(\Psi_{\mathrm{out}}\right) \) 7.00 mm, \( \Psi_{\mathrm{in}} \) 3.04 mm). The complex permeability and permittivity were computed from the experimental scattering parameters through the standard Nicolson–Ross and Weir theoretical calculations [31].
## 3 Results and discussions
## 3.1 Characterization of Co/NC nanocomposites
