Figure 2 shows the SEM images of the parent ZIF-67 and derivatives annealing at different temperatures. The parent ZIF-67 shows a typical rhombic dodecahedral shape. After Ar-annealed treatment from 500 to \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) the ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites maintained the dodecahedral morphology, while the surface became rough. By increasing the temperature, the dodecahedral skeleton collapsed slowly, and the rhombic surface was sunken. Besides, the Co nanocrystals grew bigger, which is consistent with above XRD result. Figure S2 in the ESM displays the energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectra of Co/NC nanocomposites. C, N, and Co elemental signals can be detected. The Si signal is originated from the substrate of silicon wafer. The O signal is attributed to the absorbed oxygen or surface oxidation of Co, when the Co/NC nanocomposites are exposed in air. The corresponding element analysis (Table S1 in the ESM) suggests that the relative ratio of Co/Co is decreased by increasing the pyrolysis temperature. This is mainly due to that higher temperature can induce bigger particle size of Co nanocrystals. The Co nanocrystals with smaller size are easily oxidized. Moreover, the relative proportions of Co and N are reduced.
The pyrolysis process of ZIF-67 is examined by TGA curve (Fig. S3 in the ESM). The slight weight loss before \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is related to the removal of guest molecules or absorbed molecules. This also indicates the remarkable thermal stability of ZIF-67, which is accordant with the XRD results. A sudden weight loss starts from about \(480\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) which is attributed to the decomposition of organic linkers and order-to-disorder transition in ZIF-67 [32–34]. At \(500{-}600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) a significant mass loss can be seen. Mass losses of about \(5\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) , \(35\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) , \(41\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) , \(44\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) , \(50\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) , and \(51\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) are detected at 500, 600, 700, 800, 1,000, and \(1{,}100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) respectively. When the temperature increases to \(1{,}100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) the TGA curve still shows a continuous downward trend, and no platform is observed. This variation tendency of TGA curve for ZIF-67 is widely reported in Refs. [32–34]. These phenomena are confusing,
for the largest mass loss should be only about \(30\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) (we suppose ZIF-67 with molecular formula of \(\mathrm{CoC_{8}N_{4}H_{10}}\) is completely decomposed into \(\mathrm{Co/C}\)). No weight loss should be detected in the high temperature range, especially at \(1{,}100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The specific reason is still puzzled. Based on the EDX and TGA results, we assume that \(\mathrm{Co-N}\) bonds are broken when the temperature is higher than \(500\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Some Co and N species may be lost as the temperature increases.
To identify the morphological and structural evolutions of ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites, TEM and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images were taken as shown in Fig. 3. In particular, the structural evolutions of the ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites include three aspects: morphological evolution of Co/NC nanocomposites, formation of Co nanocrystals, and phase conversion of carbon component. Figures 3(a), 3(c), 3(e), 3(g), and 3(i) show the representative TEM images of a single particle of Co/NC nanocomposites. TEM and HRTEM images of more areas for the Co/NC nanocomposites are displayed in Figs. S4–S8 in the ESM. Obviously, when the pyrolysis temperature is lower than \(800\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) the ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites show a regular dodecahedron shape, in which tiny Co nanocrystals are uniformly embedded in the carbon skeleton. At \(1{,}000\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) the structure of the Co/NC-1000 (Fig. 3(i) and Fig. S8(a) in the ESM) is collapsed into a hexagonal shape. The Co nanocrystals grow bigger as the temperature increases. The typical HRTEM image of Co nanocrystals in the Co/NC-000 (inset of Fig. S(c)) shows an interplanar spacing distance of \(0.208\,\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the (111) crystalline plane of metallic Co with FCC structure. To characterize the phase conversion of carbon component, HRTEM images of carbon are performed. No lattice fringes are observed in the carbon component of Co/NC-500 (Fig. 3(b) and Fig. S4 in the ESM), indicating that the carbon layer is amorphous. The tiny Co nanocrystals are coated by thick carbon layer. When the temperature reaches \(600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), most carbon areas in Co/NC-600 (Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S5 in the ESM) are still amorphous. The carbon layer surrounded Co nanocrystals appear a very thin graphite carbon with fingerprint-like feature. The interplanar spacing is \(0.338\,\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the (002) plane of graphite carbon. By increasing the temperature from 600 to \(1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the graphite carbon areas with fingerprint-like characteristic become larger
(Figs. 3(d), 3(f), 3(h), and 3(j), and Figs. S5–S8 in the ESM). At \(800\ ^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},\) the carbon component was completely transferred into crystalline graphite (Fig. 3(h) and Fig. S7 in the ESM). CNTs are found in Co/NC-1000 (Fig. 3(j) and Fig. S8 in the ESM). This is attributed to the graphitization effect of carbon by the catalysis of Co nanocrystals. Moreover, as the temperature increases, it seems that the Co nanocrystals move from the inside to the surface of the carbon texture (the change can be seen by comparing the surfaces in Figs. 3(b), 3(d), 3(f), 3(h), and 3(j), and Figs. S4–S8 in the ESM). For example, compared to that all Co nanocrystals are located inside of Co/NC-500 (Fig. 3(b) and Fig. S4 in the ESM), many Co nanocrystals appear at the surface of Co/NC-800 (Fig. 3(h) and Fig. S7 in the ESM). In the Co/NC-1000, many pores can be
observed at the out layer of carbon texture (Fig. 3(j) and Fig. S7 in the ESM). This is mainly due to that some Co nanocrystals at the surface of carbon texture are encapsulated or volatilized, leading to the appearance of pores. Figure 3(k) shows the representative high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image and relevant elemental mapping of Co/NC-600. Co, C, O, and N signals are found throughout the whole particle. This result indicates that Co nanocrystals are uniformly distributed in the carbon matrix. Besides, the area of Co element is a little smaller than those of C and N elements, which suggests that the Co nanocrystals are mainly encapsulated in the carbon skeleton.
In-situ heating TEM technique is adopted to further
dynamically investigate the structural evolution and formation process of ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites. The parent ZIF-67 was dispersed on a silicon nitride chip, which was placed in a protochip TEM holder for in-situ experiment. The dynamical in-situ pyrolysis process of ZIF-67 can be seen from the Video ESM1. Figure 4 shows the representative dynamic in-situ heating TEM images of ZIF-67 surface at \(600{-}1,100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The sample is kept at 600, 700, 800, 1,000, and \(1,100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), respectively. The blurry images in Figs. 4(a) and 4(b) are caused by thermal drift during the in-situ heating procedure. Compared to \(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 4(a)), some nanocrystals escaped from the carbon skeleton at \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 4(b)). At \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), more Co nanocrystals escaped out from the carbon skeleton (Fig. 4(c)). By further increasing the temperature to \(1,000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figs. 4(d) and 4(e)), the number of naked Co nanocrystals escaped out from the Co/NC-1000 nanocomposites decreased in comparison with that of \(800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 4(c)). Numerous escaped Co nanocrystals disappear when the heating temperature increases to \(1,000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figs. 4(d) and 4(e)). This is probably due to that the melting point of Co nanocrystals is tremendously reduced by the finite-size effect [35]. According to the Gibbs–Thompson relation [36], the relationship between melting point and diameter of nanoparticles can be described as \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\,=\,T_{\mathrm{nbulk}}(1\,-\,C/D)\). \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\) and \(T_{\mathrm{nbulk}}\) are the melting points of nanoparticle and bulk, respectively, \(C\) is a constant related to the material, and \(D\) is the diameter. Obviously, the melting point of nanoparticles can be greatly reduced by decreasing the particle size, especially when the diameter is smaller than \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\). Some naked tiny Co nanocrystals melt and volatilized at \(1,000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). This result directly demonstrates why the TGA curve of ZIF-67 displays a continuous downward trend with an abnormal high mass loss at \(600{-}1,100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S3 in the ESM). Combining the TGA, EDX, and in-situ heating TEM results, we surmise that some Co species (maybe some Co atoms near the surface of carbon skeleton) begin to volatilize at \(600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). A small quantity of CNTs are observed at \(1,000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 4(e)). By further increasing
the temperature to \(1,100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), more CNTs are formed by the graphitization effect of carbon at the surface, in which the escaped Co nanocrystals act as catalyst (Fig. 4(e)). HRTEM images of the escaped nanocrystals (insets of Figs. 4(b)–4(d) and 4(f)) show the (111) crystalline plane of FCC metallic Co. To visually investigate the evolution of the escaped Co nanocrystals, the particle sizes and numbers per \(100\;\mathrm{nm}^{2}\) of escaped Co nanocrystals at \(700{-}1,100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) were counted, as displayed in Fig. S9 in the ESM. Obviously, the escaped Co nanocrystals have an average particle size of about \(10\,\mathrm{nm}\), when the temperatures range from 700 to \(1,000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). When the temperature is increased to \(1,100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the particles grow bigger. In addition, the numbers of escaped Co nanocrystals increase first and then decrease. The Co/NC-800 has the largest number of escaped Co nanocrystals, while the number in the Co/NC-1100 is the lowest. These results further suggest that numerous tiny escaped Co nanocrystals volatilized at \(1,000{-}1,100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) due to the finite-size effect, while some nanocrystals grew bigger.
MOFs are a kind of electron beam sensitive materials [37–39]. The focused electron beam with high energy in TEM can damage the intrinsic structure of the MOFs, which may affects the formation of MOF derivatives. The electron irradiation effects of the focused beam, especially the heat effect, cannot be avoided in the TEM experiments. To eliminate the influence of electron irradiation effects, another in-situ heating TEM experiment without the irradiation of electron beam was performed by switching off V7 valve. The sample was in-situ heated to \(1,100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by using the TEM heating holder, in which the electron beam was closed. After the heating temperature reached \(1,000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the electron beam was open for TEM measurement. Figure S10 in the ESM shows the corresponding TEM image of the ZIF-67 derivatives, in which escaped Co nanocrystals and CNTs are observed. Besides, pores are also found, which is mainly attributed to agglomeration of Co nanocrystals caused by Ostwald ripening. This result indicates that the evolution of ZIF-67 is mainly caused by heating process.
Figure 5 shows the time-evolution of Co nanocrystals escaped from the surface of ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites under in-situ heating TEM experiment at \(800{-}900\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The typical TEM image series (Fig. 5) show that the Co nanocrystals escape from the surface of carbon skeleton, in which the nanocrystals engulf the tiny particle and grow bigger during the movement. Combining the above results of ex-situ analysis and in-situ heating TEM, four aspects can be obtained: First, the Co/NC derivatives maintain the morphology of parent ZIF-67; second, some Co nanocrystals escape from the surface of carbon skeleton during the pyrolysis treatment; third, CNTs are generated at the surface of carbon skeleton under the catalysis of Co nanocrystals; fourth, some naked tiny Co nanocrystals volatilize due to the size-dependent melting point effect, when the heating temperature increases to \(1{,}000{-}1{,}100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
XPS technique is adopted to analyze the chemical constituents and elemental valence state on the surface of Co/NC nanocomposites. The survey scan XPS spectra (Fig. S11 in the ESM) of Co/NC-600, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000 show five peaks at about 285, 400, 532, 716, and \(782\ \mathrm{eV}\), corresponding to C 1s, N 1s, O 1s, Co Auger, and Co 2p, respectively. Table S2 in the ESM summarizes the semi-quantitative elemental analysis of
Co/NC-600, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000, which is obtained from the full scan XPS spectra (Fig. S11 in the ESM). Interestingly, the Co/NC-800 displays the highest relative intensity ratio of \(\mathrm{Co/C}\). This is mainly due to that XPS is a surface sensitive technique and more Co nanocrystals are formed at the surface of Co/NC nanocomposites. The ex-situ TEM result (Fig. 3) and in-situ heating TEM experiments (Figs. 4 and 5) have proved that the Co nanocrystals move to the surface and outside when the temperature increases. However, at \(1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the part of tiny Co nanocrystals at the surface and outside of Co/NC nanocomposites volatilize, which induces the reduced intensity ratios of \(\mathrm{Co/C}\). The intensity ratio of N/C for Co/NC-800 is also the largest. This is owing to that high temperature is beneficial for the crystallization and N doping of carbon. By increasing the temperature, N element is consumed by the formation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas (Fig. S12 in the ESM), leading to the low intensity ratio of N/C in Co/NC-1000. In addition, Co/NC-1000 shows the highest intensity ratio of O/Co. For Co/NC-1000, the carbon skeleton collapses and the Co nanocrystals are exposed, which has been proved by the SEM and TEM data (Figs. 2 and 3). The surface of exposed Co nanocrystals is oxidized. The change rules of Co/C, N/C, and O/Co obtained by XPS are inconsistent with the EDX spectra (Fig. S2 and Table S1
in the ESM). This is mainly due to the different depth of detection in XPS and EDX techniques. XPS is a surface-sensitive technique, in which the low-energy photoelectrons can only enter into the samples with a very short distance \((0.5{-}3\ \mathrm{nm})\). In comparison, EDX uses X-ray with high energy as the source, which can pass through the samples with a distance of micrometer scale. Combining the EDX (Fig. S2 and Table S1 in the ESM) and XPS results (Fig. S11 and Table S2 in the ESM), we assume that Co and N elements are diffused from the inside to surface as the temperature increases, and high temperature can induce the loss of Co and N species. Figures 6(a)–6(c) show the high-resolution C 1s spectra of Co/NC-600, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000. The C 1s spectra (Figs. 6(a), 6(d), and 6(g)) can be divided into four peaks at 284.7, 285.8, 288.1, and \(289.3\;\mathrm{eV}\), which are related to the
C–C, C–N, C–O, and \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) functional groups, respectively [40]. The proportions of these four carbon-based functional groups are calculated and summarized in Table S3 in the ESM. The presence of \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}\) peak suggests that the carbon skeleton is doped by \(_\mathrm{N}\) atoms during the pyrolysis process. Co/NC-800 shows the lowest ratio of oxygen-containing groups and the highest ratio of \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}\) bond, indicating that abundant \(_\mathrm{N}\) heteroatoms are doped in the carbon lattice. The N 1s spectra (Figs. 6(b), 6(e), and \(6(\mathrm{h})\) ) can be differentiated into three species at 398.4, 399.6, and \(401.4~\mathrm{eV},\) corresponding to the pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N, respectively [40]. Co/NC-1000 shows the highest intensity ratio of graphitic N (Table S4 in the ESM), indicating that high temperature is in favor of the formation of graphitic N. The presence of \(_\mathrm{N}\) heteroatoms can provide defects and electric
polarization centers, which is conductive to the microwave absorption. The Co 2p spectra (Figs. 6(c), 6(f), and 6(i)) can be fitted by five peaks with binding energies of 779.3, 781.6, 784.5, 795.4, and \(804.1\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively. The peak at \(779.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) is assigned to the Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) of metallic Co [41]. The peaks at 781.6 and \(795.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) are attributed to the Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) of Co ions, respectively. The peaks at 784.5 and \(804.1\;\mathrm{eV}\) are the corresponding satellite peaks. The presence of Co ions is mainly due to the surface oxidation when the nanocomposites are exposed to air. The O 1s spectra (Fig. S13 in the ESM) can be fitted by three peaks at 53.0, 53.2.1, and \(533.8\;\mathrm{eV}\), which are assigned to lattice oxygen Co–O, C–O, and \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) groups, respectively. Obviously, Co/NC-800 shows the lowest intensity ratio of Co–O (Table S5 in the ESM), suggesting that the Co nanocrystals in Co/NC-800 display the best resistance to oxidation. This is mainly attributed to the protection of crystalline carbon skeleton and special encapsulated structure: Compared to Co/NC-600, the graphitization degree of carbon skeleton in Co/NC-800 is improved at higher carbonization temperature; compared to Co/NC-1000, the Co nanocrystals in Co/NC-800 are better encapsulated by the carbon species, while the carbon skeleton in Co/NC-1000 collapse and some Co nanocrystals are exposed in air without the protection of carbon skeleton.
Figure 6(j) shows the Raman spectra of Co/NC nanocomposites. The two broad peaks at about 1,350 and \(1,590\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are the characteristic D and G bands, which are attributed to the disordered and graphite carbon, respectively. As the temperature increases from 500 to \(1,000\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the intensity ratios of D and G bands \((I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})\) are obviously decreased. In addition, G bands shift to higher wavelength and D bands shift to lower wavelength. These results indicate that the degree of graphitization carbon in Co/NC nanocomposites increases by increasing the temperatures [42], which is consistent with the above XRD and TEM results. The pore structure and specific surface area of the parent ZIF-67 and Co/NC derivatives are characterized by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms, as shown in Fig. S14 in the ESM and Fig. 6(k). The parent ZIF-67 (Fig. S14(a) in the ESM) reveals a reversible type-I absorption isotherm. An abrupt rise of absorbed volume can be seen at a low pressure \((P/P_{0}<0.10)\), indicating its microporous feature. The BET surface area and microporous volume are calculated to be \(1,285\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.66\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) \((P/P_{0}=0.10–1.0)\), respectively, suggesting its highly accessible open pores in the framework structure [34]. All the Co/NC nanocomposites (Fig. 6(k)) show a representative type-IV absorption isotherm with a H4-type hysteresis loop \((P/P_{0}=0.4–1.0)\), indicating the existence of mesopores in Co/NC derivatives [43]. The BET specific surface areas of Co/NC-500, Co/NC-600, Co/NC-700, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000 are 225, 421, 352, 273, and \(114\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. Table S6 in the ESM summarizes the BET surface areas, pore volumes, and pore sizes of the parent ZIF-67 and Co/NC derivatives. The relative high BET surface areas are mainly attributed to that the carbon skeleton in Co/NC derivatives still maintains the morphology of parent ZIF-67 during the pyrolysis treatment. We repeated the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherm measurement for two times, and all results demonstrate that Co/NC-600 shows the largest BET surface area and pore volume among the Co/NC derivatives. We consider that improving the pyrolysis temperature can induce two aspects. First, it is profitable to the carbonization of organic linker in MOFs, resulting in the generation of porous carbon frameworks with large surface area. Second, it can promote the growth of Co nanocrystals, which may block the channels and reduce the surface area. We assume that the highest BET surface area of Co/NC-600 is probably resulted from these two integrated factors.
The magnetic hysteresis \((M-H)\) loops of Co/NC
nanocomposites (Fig. 6(l)) were measured at room temperature. All samples reveal a S-like shape with obvious remanence and coercivity, suggesting a ferromagnetic behavior at room temperature. The magnetic parameters of saturation magnetization \((M_{\mathrm{s}})\), magnetic remanence \((M_{\mathrm{R}})\), and coercivity \((H_{\mathrm{c}})\) are summarized in Table S7 in the ESM. The \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) values of Co/NC-500, Co/NC-600, Co/NC-700, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000 are 14.8, 67.4, 94.6, 93.3, and 88.3 emu·g\(^{-1}\), respectively. Generally, the \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) value of magnetic nanoparticles decreases by reducing the particle size, owing to the size effect and surface effect [35]. Besides, the surface oxidation and poor crystallinity can also induce surface spin disorder, resulting in reduction of \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) [26]. In this work, the particle size and crystallinity of Co nanocrystals in Co/NC nanocomposites are increased by increasing the temperature, which have been proved by the XRD and TEM data. Note that the \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) values of Co/NC nanocomposites are generally increased by elevating the temperature from 500 to \(700\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). This is mainly owing to the reduced surface oxidation, bigger particle size, and the increase of crystallinity. However, when the temperature is further improved to \(1,000\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) value of Co/NC-1000 is anomalously reduced. The abnormal reduction of \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) in Co/NC-1000 is attributed to the volatilization and loss of Co species, which has been proved by the in-situ TEM heating result.
## 3.2 Microwave absorption properties of Co/NC nanocomposites
As a kind of magnetic carbon-based composites, ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites maintain the dieleached shape with high specific surface area, in which magnetic Co nanocrystals are uniformly anchored in the carbon skeleton without aggregation. This feature can sufficiently emerge the dielectric loss and magnetic loss capacities, leading to a boosting microwave absorption performance. In order to measure the microwave absorption property, the Co/NC nanocomposites were uniformly mixed with paraffin at different mass fractions. Figure S15 in the ESM shows the electromagnetic parameters of paraffin-based microwave absorbers with \(30\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) of Co/NC nanocomposites. The \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values (Figs. S15(a) and S15(b) in the ESM) decrease as the frequency increases, showing a frequency dispersion feature. This is due to the improved hysteresis of dipole-polarization interaction, which results from the alteration of electric field caused by increasing frequencies [44]. Both \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values are improved as the temperature increases. This is originated from the transformation of carbon skeleton from amorphous to crystalline under the catalysis of Co nanocrystals. The \(\mu^{\prime}\) and \(\mu^{\prime\prime}\) values for all Co/NC nanocomposites display the same variation tendency, which vibrate as the frequency increases (Figs. S15(c) and S15(d) in the ESM).
Typically, reflection loss (RL) is used to evaluate the microwave absorption efficiency of absorbents. Especially, RL values smaller than \(-10\) dB are analogous to \(90\%\) absorption of incident microwaves. According to the transmit line theory, the RL curves of an absorber backed by a metal plate can be calculated according to Eqs. (1) and (2) [45]
\[
\mathrm{RL}(\mathrm{dB})=-20\log\left|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_0}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_0}\right|
\]
\[
Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_0(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}})^{1/2}\operatorname{tanh}\left\{\int(2\pi f\mathrm{d}/c)(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}})^{1/2}\right\}
\]
where \(Z_{\mathrm{in}},\;Z_{0},\;f,\;d,\) and \(c\) are the input impedance of absorber, impedance of air, frequency of the electromagnetic wave, absorber thickness, and velocity of light in vacuum, respectively. To satisfy the practical applications, four factors should be comprehensively
considered for the design of high-performance microwave absorbents, including optimal RL value \(\left(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\right)\), effective absorption bandwidth (EAB, \(\mathrm{RL} \leq -10 \, \mathrm{dB}\)), absorber thickness, and mass fraction.
Figure 7 shows the three-dimensional (3D) representations of RL values for the paraffin-based microwave absorbers with 30 wt.% of Co/NC nanocomposites. All the Co/NC nanocomposites show good microwave absorption performance with \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value smaller than \(-10 \, \mathrm{dB}\) and broad EAB at a relative thin matching thickness. Co/NC-500 shows a minimum RL \(\left(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\right)\) value of \(-15.0 \, \mathrm{dB}\) at \(13.85 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) with an EAB of \(4.25 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) \((13.75{-}18 \, \mathrm{GHz})\) when the absorber thickness is \(2.24 \, \mathrm{mm}\). For Co/NC-600, when the absorber thickness is only \(2.14 \, \mathrm{mm}\), an optimal RL value of \(-73.0 \, \mathrm{dB}\) can be achieved at \(13.60 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) and the corresponding EAB is \(5.46 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) \((10.53{-}15.99 \, \mathrm{GHz})\). The \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of Co/NC-700 reaches \(-71.5 \, \mathrm{dB}\) and the EAB is \(4.90 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) \((10.01{-}14.91 \, \mathrm{GHz})\) with a thickness of \(2.24 \, \mathrm{mm}\). For Co/NC-800, the \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value is \(-29.54 \, \mathrm{dB}\) at \(15.36 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) with a corresponding EAB of \(5.28 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) \((12.72{-}18 \, \mathrm{GHz})\), but the absorber thickness is only \(1.71 \, \mathrm{mm}\). While Co/NC-1000 shows a \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of \(-14.59 \, \mathrm{dB}\) at \(13.82 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) and EAB of \(3.63 \, \mathrm{GHz}\) \((12.45{-}16.08 \, \mathrm{GHz})\) with an ultrathin thickness of \(1.58 \, \mathrm{mm}\). Figure 7(f) summarizes the variation of optimal RL values and corresponding EAB of the Co/NC nanocomposites, as well as the absorber thickness. We consider that the high-efficient microwave absorption performance of ZIF-67 derived Co/NC nanocomposites is attributed to the combined benefits of porous structure, good impedance matching, and synergistic effect between Co nanocrystals and N-doped carbon skeleton.
To acquire high-efficient microwave absorption properties, the combination effects of good impedance matching \((Z = |Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|)\) and high microwave attenuation constants \((\alpha)\) should be simultaneously satisfied. The impedance matching identifies if the incident microwaves can adequately access inside of the microwave absorber. If \(Z\) is closed to 1, it means that more incident microwaves enter into the microwave absorber and fewer microwaves are reflected at the surface of absorber. For practical applications, the RL values should be smaller than \(-10 \, \mathrm{dB}\). Eq. (1) can be described as Eq. (3)
\[
\mathrm{RL}(\mathrm{dB})=-20\log\left|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_0}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_0}\right|\leqslant-10\mathrm{dB}
\]
Therefore, for practical applications, the effective \(Z\) values \((Z_{\mathrm{eff}})\) can be described as Eq. (4) [46]
\[
0.52\leqslant Z_{\mathrm{eff}}=|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_0|\leqslant1.92
\]
Figure 8 shows the two-dimensional (2D) color maps of RL and \(Z\) values for the paraffin-based microwave absorbers with \(30 \, \mathrm{wt.}\%\) of the Co/NC nanocomposites. The area of RL with values smaller than \(-10 \, \mathrm{dB}\) is smaller than the corresponding \(Z_{\mathrm{eff}}\) area. This is due to that only part of the entered microwaves is absorbed by the active materials and the other part of entered microwaves is reflected into the space without consumption. Moreover, the variation tendency of \(Z_{\mathrm{eff}}\) areas for the Co/NC nanocomposites is \(\mathrm{Co/NC-500 > Co/NC-600 > Co/NC-700 > Co/NC-800 > Co/NC-1000}\). It is mainly attributed to that the dielectric loss increases as the pyrolysis temperature increases, resulting in the impedance mismatching between magnetic loss and dielectric loss.
The other important factor is microwave attenuation capacity, which is used to evaluate the consumption ability of the entered microwaves through magnetic loss and dielectric loss of the absorber. The microwave attenuation constants \((\alpha)\) can be obtained by Eq. (5) [47]
\[
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2\pi f}}{c}\times\sqrt{(\mu''\varepsilon''-\mu'\varepsilon')+\sqrt{(\mu''\varepsilon''-\mu'\varepsilon')^2+(\varepsilon''\mu''+\varepsilon''\mu')^2}}
\]
Figure 9 shows the microwave attenuation constants of the paraffin-based microwave absorbers with \(30 \, \mathrm{wt.}\%\) of the Co/NC nanocomposites. Obviously, Co/NC-1000 has the highest \(\alpha\) value in the entire frequency range, which is probably due to the high values of \(\varepsilon'\). To evaluate the dielectric and magnetic loss capacities of the Co/NC nanocomposites, the dielectric loss tangent \((\tan \delta_{\varepsilon} = \varepsilon' / \varepsilon')\) and magnetic loss tangent \((\tan \delta_{\mu} = \mu'' / \mu')\) curves are displayed in Fig. S16 in the ESM. By increasing the pyrolysis temperature, the \(\tan \delta_{\varepsilon}\) values are improved, owing to the formation of graphite carbon at higher temperature [48]. The Cole–cole curves (Figs. S17(a)–S17(e) in the ESM) indicate that polarization effects and conduction loss play the main role in higher frequency range \((2.0{-}18.0 \, \mathrm{GHz})\) and lower frequency range \((0.1{-}2.0 \, \mathrm{GHz})\), respectively [49, 50]. As the pyrolysis temperature increases, conduction loss is dominating, which broadens the frequency range. This is attributed to that the formed graphite carbon boosts the migration of electrons under alternating electromagnetic field. For the \(\tan \delta_{\mu}\) values, the Co/NC nanocomposites show ignored differences, which is probably due to the low weight ratio of magnetic Co in the nanocomposites.
Especially, Co/NC-600 shows slightly large \(\tan\delta_{\mu}\) values in the higher frequency range of about \(10{-}18\,\mathrm{GHz}\), although it displays a relative lower \(M_{\mathrm{s}}\) value. The \(C_{0}\) curves (Fig. S17(f) in the ESM) suggest that the magnetic loss is caused by natural resonance at lower frequency range and eddy current loss at higher frequencies range [51]. The \(\tan\delta_{\varepsilon}\) values are larger than the \(\tan\delta_{\mu}\) values. This suggests that the dielectric loss plays a primary role to consume the entered microwaves.
The pyrolysis temperature, heating rate, and mass fraction of active microwave absorption materials in the microwave absorbers can greatly determine the electromagnetic parameters, which can affect the microwave absorption performance. Figures S18–S25 in the ESM show the frequency dependence of electromagnetic parameters and 3D RL maps of paraffin-based microwave absorbers with different mass fractions of Co/NC-600, Co/NC800, Co/NC-1000, and Co/NC-1100. For all the Co/NC nanocomposites, the complex permittivity decreases as the mass fraction decreases, while the change of complex permeability can be ignored. Co/NC-600 shows a remarkable microwave absorption performance when the mass fraction is \(30\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\), which behaves an \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of \(-73.0\) dB at \(13.60\ \mathrm{GHz}\) with the corresponding EAB of \(5.46\ \mathrm{GHz}\) at \(2.14~\mathrm{mm}\) (Figs. S18 and S19 in the ESM). Co/NC-800 performs the optimal microwave absorption properties with a mass fraction of \(20~\mathrm{wt.\%}\) (Figs. S20 and S21 in the ESM). The minimum RL value can reach \(-62.73\) dB at \(10.86~\mathrm{GHz}\) and the EAB is \(4.26~\mathrm{GHz}\) \((8.71{-}12.97~\mathrm{GHz})\), when the thickness is \(2.62\ \mathrm{mm}\). For Co/NC-1000, the optimal
mass fraction is \(15~\mathrm{wt.\%}\) (Figs. S22 and S23 in the ESM). The minimum RL value is \(-31.52\) dB at \(11.89\ \mathrm{GHz}\) and EAB is \(4.72\:\mathrm{GHz}\) \((9.14{-}13.86\,\mathrm{GHz})\) with a thickness of \(2.96\;\mathrm{mm}\). Co/NC1100 behaves the superior microwave absorption performance when the mass fraction is \(20\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) (Figs. S24 and S25 in the ESM). The \(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}\) value is \(-76.33\;\mathrm{dB}\) at \(10.86\,\mathrm{GHz}\) with EAB of \(4.57\:\mathrm{GHz}\), but the absorber thickness is \(3.46~\mathrm{mm}\). In addition, we also study the influence of heating rates on the morphology, electromagnetic parameters, and microwave absorption of Co/NC nanocomposites. Taking Co/NC-600 as the example, the nanocomposites were fabricated at 2, 5 and \(10\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), respectively. XRD patterns (Fig. S26 in the ESM) and SEM images (Fig. S27 in the ESM) indicate that the heating rate has little influence on the crystalline phase and skeleton structure of Co/NC nanocomposites. The only distinction is that more Co nanocrystals are present at the surface of carbon skeleton by increasing the heating rate. As the heating rate increases, the complex permittivity and real part of complex permeability are reduced (Fig. S28 in the ESM). Simultaneously, the microwave absorption performance becomes worse (Fig. S29 in the ESM). These results indicate that the microwave absorption performance can be controlled by changing the mass fraction of Co/NC nanocomposites, pyrolysis temperature, and heating rate. Table S8 in the ESM summarizes the comparison of microwave absorption parameters between Co/NC nanocomposites and traditional ferrites or magnetic oxides based composites. The Co/NC nanocomposites show high-efficient microwave absorption performance and competitive broad EAB in comparison with the traditional ferrites or magnetic oxides based composites, but the mass fraction and matching thickness are relatively lower.
With the wide popularization of electronic devices, the anti-oxygenic and anti-corrosive qualities are of great significance for the practical applications of microwave absorbers. Figure S30 in the ESM shows the XRD patterns of Co/NC-600, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000 nanocomposites annealed in air atmosphere at different temperatures. The Co nanocrystals in Co/NC-600 and Co/NC-1000 are chemically converted into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) phase at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The Co/NC-800 reveals an improved oxidation resistance, which is converted into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) when the annealing temperature is increased to \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Figure S31 in the ESM displays the optical photographs of Co/NC-600, Co/NC-800, and Co/NC-1000 nanocomposites which were immersed in \(0.1\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) HCl solution for different times. Only the acid solution containing Co/NC-800 maintains colorless after \(48~\mathrm{h}\), indicating that Co/NC-800 shows the best corrosion resistance. These phenomena indicate that Co/NC-800 performs the superior corrosion and oxidation
resistance. Crystalline carbon skeleton and special encapsulated texture may be responsible for the chemical stability. For Co/NC-1000, the dodecahedral carbon frameworks collapse and many Co nanocrystals are exposed on the surface of carbon skeleton, which is easy to be oxidized and corroded. For both Co/NC-600 and Co/NC-800, Co nanocrystals are mainly encapsulated in the carbon skeleton. However, compared to the amorphous carbon in Co/NC-600, the crystalline carbon in Co/NC-800 is more effective to protect Co nanocrystals from corrosion and oxidation. This result suggests that Co/NC-800 is potential materials for the construction of high-efficiency microwave absorbents with superior corrosion and oxidation resistance.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, taking Co-based ZIF-67 as the example, the formation process of MOFs derived carbon-based nanocomposites is dynamically investigated by in-situ heating TEM technique and ex-situ pyrolysis process. The main findings are as follows: (1) Co nanocrystals are uniformly encapsulated in the dodecahedral carbon skeleton when the pyrolysis temperature is below \(1{,}000\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\); (2) catalyzed by Co nanocrystals, the amorphous carbon texture is converted into crystalline phase at \(600\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and completely becomes crystalline at \(800\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\); (3) at \(700\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), some Co nanocrystals move out and escape from the carbon skeleton, which is especially evident at \(800\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\); (4) when the temperature reaches \(1{,}000\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), carbon nanotubes are formed with the catalysis of Co nanocrystals; (5) the tiny Co nanocrystals which escape out from the carbon skeleton are volatilized and disappear when the temperature increases to \(700{-}1{,}100\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Moreover, the Co/N-doped carbon nanocomposites show high-efficient microwave absorption performance, which can be controlled by tuning the pyrolysis temperature, heating rate, and mass fraction. This work provides an insight into the understanding of the formation of MOFs derivatives, which should be significant to expand their practical applications.
